1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.002
In this lesson, you will extend

2
00:00:02.002 --> 00:00:04.471
the knowledge graph with business process data

3
00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:06.473
to better connect API services

4
00:00:06.473 --> 00:00:09.776
and provide context about their dependencies

5
00:00:09.776 --> 00:00:13.480
in the processes. All right, let's dive in.

6
00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:16.850
this notebook, we will include process data into our knowledge graph

7
00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.551
to connect the APIs.

8
00:00:18.551 --> 00:00:20.553
As you remember from the previous lesson,

9
00:00:20.553 --> 00:00:22.555
the two APIs were disconnected,

10
00:00:22.555 --> 00:00:25.158
and by the end of the notebook, you will have added

11
00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:28.862
these red nodes to the graph that will connect the APIs

12
00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:30.864
by the business process information.

13
00:00:31.231 --> 00:00:35.068
In the simplified business process schema, there are two main concepts:

14
00:00:35.702 --> 00:00:39.739
processes and activities. A process defines a collection of activities

15
00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:43.410
which are executed in a specific order to complete the process.

16
00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:46.813
A process has a name, description, and a version.

17
00:00:47.113 --> 00:00:50.316
A process links to the start activity of that process.

18
00:00:50.517 --> 00:00:53.420
Each activity has a name and a description,

19
00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:55.822
and it links to the next activity in the process.

20
00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:58.625
To link the process graph data to the APIs,

21
00:00:58.625 --> 00:01:02.629
we can link activities to entity sets wherever applicable.

22
00:01:02.629 --> 00:01:06.132
For example, the activity create purchase requisition

23
00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:09.335
can be linked to the purchase requisition entity set.

24
00:01:09.335 --> 00:01:11.938
For simplicity, we omit gateways

25
00:01:11.938 --> 00:01:15.909
and merely focus on simple processes which are sequences in this lesson.

26
00:01:16.342 --> 00:01:18.545
Now, let's take a look at an example.

27
00:01:18.845 --> 00:01:22.515
Consider a simple process for the procurement of direct materials.

28
00:01:22.816 --> 00:01:25.418
This process is typically executed by a purchaser.

29
00:01:25.819 --> 00:01:27.921
First, a purchase requisition is created.

30
00:01:28.321 --> 00:01:30.457
After it is approved, a new

31
00:01:30.457 --> 00:01:32.926
purchase order is created from this requisition.

32
00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:37.831
Finally, after some potential intermediate steps, the purchase order is closed.

33
00:01:38.098 --> 00:01:42.268
The process steps to create a purchase requisition and order

34
00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:45.905
can be linked to the corresponding EntitySets in the knowledge graph.

35
00:01:46.206 --> 00:01:49.109
From this connection, an agent can derive

36
00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:51.978
that the purchase order EntitySet depends on

37
00:01:51.978 --> 00:01:53.880
the purchase requisition

38
00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.583
in the context of this specific process.

39
00:01:57.117 --> 00:02:00.620
Now, let's jump into the notebook and see how we can integrate

40
00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.123
this process data with the API data

41
00:02:03.123 --> 00:02:05.925
and the knowledge graph we created in the previous lesson.

42
00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:09.429
Again, we start with importing the necessary packages

43
00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:11.397
and these packages correspond to the same

44
00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:13.633
packages that we used in the previous lesson.

45
00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:15.602
Now that you want to link the

46
00:02:15.602 --> 00:02:17.237
process data to your business API knowledge graph

47
00:02:17.237 --> 00:02:19.639
from the previous lesson,

48
00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:24.043
you can start by importing this knowledge graph from the previous lesson.

49
00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.813
Here, we first set up an empty graph

50
00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:28.781
called the API knowledge graph.

51
00:02:29.082 --> 00:02:31.484
We then read the file that we materialized

52
00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:33.786
in the previous lesson and parse it,

53
00:02:34.087 --> 00:02:35.655
and finally we print the number

54
00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:37.490
of triples that are in this graph.

55
00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:40.560
The result is over 14000 triples.

56
00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.862
This number should correspond

57
00:02:42.862 --> 00:02:45.565
to the size of the knowledge graph in the previous lesson.

58
00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:47.967
For this lesson, we already extracted

59
00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:50.970
some processes from BPMN diagrams

60
00:02:51.271 --> 00:02:52.972
and converted them into RDF.

61
00:02:53.573 --> 00:02:56.576
BPMN or the Business Process Model Notation

62
00:02:56.576 --> 00:02:58.945
is a standard for defining business processes.

63
00:02:59.379 --> 00:03:01.548
Let's also load this process graph

64
00:03:01.548 --> 00:03:05.118
from a turtle file. Similar to the previous step,

65
00:03:05.118 --> 00:03:07.220
we create an empty process knowledge graph,

66
00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:10.123
we parse the business processes from a turtle file,

67
00:03:10.123 --> 00:03:13.126
and then we print the size of the business process graph.

68
00:03:13.126 --> 00:03:17.230
Our business process graph has around 150 triples.

69
00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:20.600
So let's take a look at the processes in that graph.

70
00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:23.970
Here, we use a SPARQL query to

71
00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.839
retrieve the processes from the Business Process Graph.

72
00:03:27.473 --> 00:03:29.375
In this query, we select any

73
00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:31.578
process and the name of the process.

74
00:03:32.078 --> 00:03:34.514
Finally, we just project the name of the process.

75
00:03:35.148 --> 00:03:37.684
We run this query over the process

76
00:03:37.684 --> 00:03:40.620
knowledge graph and print the processes by iterating

77
00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:43.623
over all results and just printing the name of the process.

78
00:03:43.957 --> 00:03:45.725
So let's take a look at

79
00:03:45.725 --> 00:03:47.727
what processes are in the knowledge graph.

80
00:03:48.127 --> 00:03:50.129
Here you see the result.

81
00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:53.733
The processes include a process for the Procurement of Direct Materials,

82
00:03:53.933 --> 00:03:56.836
Sell from Stock, External Transportation Planning,

83
00:03:56.936 --> 00:03:58.638
and so on and so forth.

84
00:03:58.738 --> 00:04:00.306
This corresponds to processes that you

85
00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:02.141
would find in most of the companies.

86
00:04:02.709 --> 00:04:05.545
Now that we've seen some of the processes,

87
00:04:05.545 --> 00:04:08.514
we can simply add the Business Process Graph

88
00:04:08.514 --> 00:04:10.516
to the Business API Graph from the previous lesson

89
00:04:10.516 --> 00:04:12.619
to get a combined knowledge graph.

90
00:04:12.852 --> 00:04:15.455
To do so, we again set up an empty graph

91
00:04:15.955 --> 00:04:17.857
and then we just combine the two graphs,

92
00:04:17.957 --> 00:04:19.692
the API knowledge graph and the

93
00:04:19.692 --> 00:04:21.728
process knowledge graph into a single graph.

94
00:04:22.128 --> 00:04:24.530
And we print the size of the graph to see

95
00:04:24.631 --> 00:04:26.633
whether everything worked as expected.

96
00:04:27.233 --> 00:04:29.435
As you can see, the resulting knowledge graph

97
00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:33.039
is exactly the size of the individual knowledge graphs.

98
00:04:33.039 --> 00:04:35.742
The API knowledge graph and the process knowledge graph.

99
00:04:36.042 --> 00:04:38.044
Similar to the previous lesson, you can

100
00:04:38.044 --> 00:04:40.046
serialize the knowledge graph in turtle syntax

101
00:04:40.046 --> 00:04:41.748
and write the file to disk.

102
00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:44.751
After combining the graphs, we can query the knowledge graph

103
00:04:44.851 --> 00:04:47.754
and find connections between processes and APIs.

104
00:04:48.154 --> 00:04:50.156
In the process graph, activities can refer

105
00:04:50.256 --> 00:04:53.259
to the corresponding API entity sets if applicable.

106
00:04:53.326 --> 00:04:55.828
Here you see a SPARQL query

107
00:04:55.828 --> 00:04:59.832
to retrieve the activities of the Procurement of Direct Materials

108
00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:02.135
and the activities that are linked

109
00:05:02.135 --> 00:05:04.137
to the entity sets.

110
00:05:04.337 --> 00:05:08.141
First, retrieve the process of Procurement of Direct Materials.

111
00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:11.144
We find the start activity of that process

112
00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:13.980
Then we use a property path query

113
00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:17.984
to get all next activities of that start activity.

114
00:05:18.284 --> 00:05:22.488
We then see if that activity is linked to an entity set,

115
00:05:22.689 --> 00:05:25.491
and if so, we retrieve the name of that entity set

116
00:05:25.692 --> 00:05:27.593
as well as the name of the

117
00:05:27.593 --> 00:05:29.495
service the entity set is belonging to.

118
00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:32.432
Again, we run the query over the combined graph

119
00:05:32.432 --> 00:05:35.435
and just print the results to see the activities,

120
00:05:35.435 --> 00:05:37.637
the entity set they are connected to,

121
00:05:37.637 --> 00:05:39.939
as well as the service of that entity set.

122
00:05:40.306 --> 00:05:42.809
Here we see the activity

123
00:05:42.809 --> 00:05:46.813
Create Purchase Requisition is connected to the Entity Set Purchase Requisition

124
00:05:46.813 --> 00:05:49.282
in the Service API_PURCHASEREQUISITION_2.

125
00:05:49.482 --> 00:05:52.085
The activity Create Purchase Order is connected

126
00:05:52.085 --> 00:05:53.786
to the Entity Set PurchaseOrder

127
00:05:53.786 --> 00:05:55.488
in the purchase order API.

128
00:05:56.189 --> 00:05:58.191
Let's take another look at the disconnected

129
00:05:58.191 --> 00:06:00.460
graph that we saw in the previous lesson.

130
00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:03.663
As you can see, the APIs were disconnected.

131
00:06:04.063 --> 00:06:07.767
There's no connection between the purchase requisition API information

132
00:06:07.867 --> 00:06:10.169
and the purchase order API information.

133
00:06:10.703 --> 00:06:14.407
Now you will see how these services are connected via the processes.

134
00:06:14.407 --> 00:06:16.509
Just like in the previous lesson,

135
00:06:16.509 --> 00:06:18.811
you first get the nodes from the knowledge graph.

136
00:06:18.811 --> 00:06:22.615
Now, however, you only need either the purchase order node

137
00:06:22.615 --> 00:06:25.818
or the purchase requisition node as the starting entity set.

138
00:06:25.818 --> 00:06:28.821
This is because the entity sets are now reachable

139
00:06:28.821 --> 00:06:31.924
from each other via this process information.

140
00:06:32.525 --> 00:06:35.428
So, again, like in the previous lesson,

141
00:06:35.428 --> 00:06:37.630
we retrieve the entity set as a starting point.

142
00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:42.235
In this case, we only look for the purchase order entity set.

143
00:06:42.635 --> 00:06:45.238
Here's the result of the query. We retrieve the URI,

144
00:06:45.238 --> 00:06:47.073
the unique identifier of the purchase

145
00:06:47.073 --> 00:06:49.242
order entity set from the knowledge graph.

146
00:06:49.475 --> 00:06:51.577
Now you can plot the connected graph

147
00:06:51.577 --> 00:06:54.280
just by taking the purchase order entity set URI

148
00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:56.282
as the seed node for the graph.

149
00:06:56.682 --> 00:06:58.651
Similar to the previous lesson,

150
00:06:58.751 --> 00:07:00.720
again, we convert the RDF graph

151
00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.955
to a networkx graph to be visualized.

152
00:07:03.423 --> 00:07:06.325
We then construct the subgraph induced

153
00:07:06.325 --> 00:07:08.094
by the purchase order node that

154
00:07:08.094 --> 00:07:10.196
we just retrieved from the knowledge graph.

155
00:07:10.596 --> 00:07:13.599
Again, we want to print and visualize the graph.

156
00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:17.603
We first define different node colors for the different types of nodes.

157
00:07:17.904 --> 00:07:19.605
Again, also different shapes

158
00:07:19.906 --> 00:07:22.208
and the sizes of the nodes should be differently,

159
00:07:22.408 --> 00:07:25.211
just in order to be more distinguishable by each other.

160
00:07:25.812 --> 00:07:29.749
Now you can see the resulting graph which is connected.

161
00:07:30.116 --> 00:07:35.021
Again, in blue is the API information about the purchase requisition API.

162
00:07:35.421 --> 00:07:39.025
In gray, the API information about the purchase order API.

163
00:07:39.325 --> 00:07:42.728
And shown in red here are the nodes, the activities

164
00:07:42.829 --> 00:07:45.264
from the business processes that allow us to

165
00:07:45.264 --> 00:07:47.700
connect the entity sets for the purchase order

166
00:07:47.834 --> 00:07:49.602
and the purchase requisition.

167
00:07:49.936 --> 00:07:52.438
Finally, after creating the knowledge graph

168
00:07:52.438 --> 00:07:55.741
combining API information and business process information

169
00:07:55.741 --> 00:07:59.712
and seeing how they are connected via the process activities,

170
00:07:59.712 --> 00:08:02.415
let's take a look at the entire knowledge graph.

171
00:08:02.782 --> 00:08:05.351
While the model or the schema is rather simple

172
00:08:05.351 --> 00:08:07.787
and only consists of the few concepts,

173
00:08:07.787 --> 00:08:10.590
the resulting knowledge graph with instance data,

174
00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:13.292
so the actual API definitions and processes

175
00:08:13.292 --> 00:08:15.294
is much larger and complex.

176
00:08:15.695 --> 00:08:18.898
So we only will visualize a subset of the graph

177
00:08:18.898 --> 00:08:21.501
with a thousand randomly chosen edges.

178
00:08:21.501 --> 00:08:23.903
This SPARQL query retrieves any edge

179
00:08:23.903 --> 00:08:25.505
from the graph,

180
00:08:25.505 --> 00:08:29.208
orders them randomly and selects just 1000 of those edges.

181
00:08:29.208 --> 00:08:31.911
And we add the resulting edges to an edges_list.

182
00:08:32.245 --> 00:08:35.548
Just like before, we use networkx to plot the graph.

183
00:08:35.848 --> 00:08:37.950
we add the edges we sampled from

184
00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:40.052
the knowledge graph and show the graph.

185
00:08:40.353 --> 00:08:43.756
This figure illustrates the size and complexity of the knowledge graph.

186
00:08:44.056 --> 00:08:46.459
And it even only shows a thousand edges,

187
00:08:46.459 --> 00:08:49.262
which corresponds to only 6% of the whole graph.

188
00:08:49.595 --> 00:08:53.432
As you see, the entire knowledge graph is too large and complex,

189
00:08:53.533 --> 00:08:55.835
making it infeasible to be provided as

190
00:08:55.835 --> 00:08:58.137
context to an agent in its entirety.

191
00:08:58.437 --> 00:09:00.840
In the next lesson, you will learn how to discover

192
00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:04.410
only relevant APIs from the knowledge graph using semantic embeddings.
